    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i've also been reading about the critiques of it. people seem to say originalism is just a way for judges to push their own political agendas under the guise of \"textualism.\" how do scholars usually respond to the idea that it's basically just a tool for conservative politics?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Scholars counter this critique by arguing that the judicial method itself remains neutral regardless of the political leanings of the judge applying it; the political bias would lie in the specific *meanings* found in the text, not the *method* of interpretation. Additionally, the \"bright line\" rules often cited by critics are sometimes actually more flexible than proponents assume, as originalists must often grapple with complex terms like \"religion\" that are not inherently conservative. Furthermore, many scholars point out that originalism is not inherently a \"conservative\" tool, as it was also adopted by liberal justices and applied to cases that protected minority rights in ways that later liberal interpretation might not support. Essentially, the debate is often a false dichotomy between a \"neutral\" method and a \"partisan\" ideology, where the partisan nature is actually inherent in all interpretive approaches.",
      "source": "target",
